  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Patchy fog this morning. \n\n- Warm, dry week ahead for the region.\n\n- Showers return late next week or early next weekend.\n\nThe low pressure system that brought rain over the last couple\ndays is moving east across Wyoming this morning. In its wake, high\npressure will build over the Northern Rockies today. Recent \nrainfall combined with decreasing cloud cover and stable \nconditions the next few days will cause patchy fog in many of the \nvalleys of central Idaho and western Montana this morning. The \nfog is expected to clear out by this afternoon due to drier air \nfrom aloft mixing down in the afternoon. \n\nAnother low pressure circulation is moving into the U.S. West \nCoast and heading south of the desert southwest. There's also a \nbroad trough established across the northern Pacific Ocean. The \ntwo features combined will setup a southwest, split flow pattern \ninto the northern Rockies region. As a result, the region will see\nwarm and mild conditions most of the week. Afternoon temperatures\nwill be about 10 degrees above normal with light winds and partly\ncloudy skies. \n\nAs the broad trough over the northern Pacific starts to progress\neastward, it will start to bring showers back to the region late\nnext week or early next weekend. With the southwest flow pattern,\nthe snow levels will remain very high and mountain snow isn't a\nconcern yet, however, as we get into the week after if the trough\ncontinues to progress eastward it would bring some cooler air into\nthe region. So the bottom line is expect a warm, mild week with\nsome showers moving in next weekend and the potential for more\nmountain snow a week or more away.",
